<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pierre Werner</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1913–2002</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pierre Werner was a Luxembourg Christian Social politician who served as prime minister from 1959 to 1974 and again from 1979 to 1984. In 1970 he chaired the group that produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werner-report">Werner Report</a>, the first plan for economic and monetary union in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-communities">European Communities</a>, which is why he is often called a father of the euro. His long tenure saw the diversification of Luxembourg's economy into banking and services.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-werner.jpg" alt="Pierre Werner" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marcel Tockert · CC BY-SA 3.0 lu · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pierre_Werner_204g.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
